attributes.probed_form:
THREE distinct axes were probed against the surviving 2.0f/0.5f transposition; all three are DO-NOT-RETRY. (A) MOVE THE RATIO BLOCK AHEAD OF THE CENTERS, the form the pool order literally implies. Literal text, replacing the vpcx/vpcy block in pbProjCalc:
    ratio = (g->current->aspect * fw) / fh;
    if ((((PBSCREEN*) g->screen)->flags & 2) != 0) {
        ratio = ratio * 2.0f;
    }
    vpcx = 0.5f * ((pl + pr) - sw);
    vpcy = 0.5f * ((pt + pb) - sh);
    vpcx = vpcx + ((PBSCREEN*) g->screen)->xoff;
    vpcy = vpcy + ((PBSCREEN*) g->screen)->yoff;
RESULT: pool order becomes EXACT (all 20 entries, 0x70, byte-identical) but pbProjCalc regresses 70 -> 108 differing words, because MWCC then EMITS the ratio divide early while the target emits it late: our +0x354 block appears at target +0x3b8 and the target's +0x354 block appears at our +0x390 - a clean two-block swap, i.e. the target's TEXT order is our UNMOVED source order. Pool order and text order therefore demand opposite source arrangements under this form. (B) UNINSTANTIATED static inline AS THE ORDERING DEVICE - `static inline void pbWindowDefaults(void)` instead of `static void`. RESULT: NEGATIVE and informative - .sdata2 fell straight back to 0x78 and .text back to 0x1874, so an inline that is never expanded creates NO pool entries at all. Pool entries come only from functions MWCC actually code-generates. (C) DIVISION FORM FOR THE CENTERS, on the theory that MWCC parses the 2.0f divisor and then strength-reduces to a 0.5f multiply, creating the two literals in that order with identical emitted code:
    vpcx = ((pl + pr) - sw) / 2.0f;
    vpcy = ((pt + pb) - sh) / 2.0f;
RESULT: NEGATIVE on both counts - pool order UNCHANGED (0.5f still precedes 2.0f, so no 2.0f literal is created at parse) and pbProjCalc 70 -> 72 differing words.
